# Maintainer reconciliation

Use this runbook for branch/PR housekeeping. Mathematical authority and review remain
in [STATE.md](STATE.md) and [REVIEW_POLICY.md](REVIEW_POLICY.md). A merged artifact
never implies canonical promotion.

Issue [#986](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/issues/986) has an
[exact-source preservation ledger](maintenance/issue-986-preservation.json).
It distinguishes a complete successor mapping, a preserved noncanonical result,
an open-PR predecessor and an unresolved source review. The seven pentagon
automation sources have a compact
[angular/contact-debt consolidation](reports/target_c_pentagon_activation_and_contact_debt_preservation_2026-10-15.md).
Their scheduler ownership remains unverified; preserving their mathematics does
not authorize deleting an operationally uncertain ref. The ledger records why
the absent PR #624 report and the relocated inactive-diameter note need no
duplicate import: use their corrected canonical successors at the stated scopes.
The [open-cell finite preservation note](reports/open_cell_staircase_finite_preservation_2026-10-15.md)
restores a missing checker while leaving the existing all-size/geometric
candidates unreviewed. The outer-nearunit source also proposed two IDs absent
from the live manifest: its shared-witness charge and fixed-anchor regression
remain in their existing unreviewed source report, not in the canonical stack.

## Read-only inventory

Fetch before collecting. Keep inventories, recovery objects, approval records and
worktree patches in durable private storage outside any release candidate.

```sh
git fetch origin --prune
python3 tools/branch_lifecycle.py --output /private/audit/branches.json \
  --save-snapshot /private/audit/github.json --tsv /private/audit/branches.tsv \
  --deletion-manifest /private/audit/deletion-preview.json
```

Replace `/private/audit` with an existing private directory. The tool paginates all
branches and **all PR states**; it does not fetch, change refs, post messages or delete.
Local `origin/main` must match the observed remote main. Missing commits, shallow
history, duplicate records and API failures stop the audit. A saved `--snapshot` is a
dated observation, never evidence of current activity.

Automatic categories are A (ancestor of main), E (protected) and F (unclear). Main,
open PR heads/bases, registered worktrees including detached tips and explicit
`--protect-ref` entries are protected. Automation refs remain ineligible and unclear
until their operational use is established; a prefix does not confirm activity. Even missing/prunable
worktree registrations remain protected until separately inspected. Duplicate tips
are reported; sharing a tip alone does not authorize deletion.

B (content represented), C (superseded) and D (salvage required) require a file-level
review. A merged PR label alone does not establish that its current branch tip is
integrated: post-merge commits may contain unique work. Compare exact report/checker/
data blobs, historical main objects, corrected successors and failed-route evidence.
Record source refs/SHAs and precise retained paths. Never substitute patch counts,
branch age, titles or passing computation for this review.

## Preservation and deletion

Before removing unique history, create a complete private snapshot, verify it and test
restoration. For example, from the fetched archive:

```sh
git bundle create /private/audit/recovery.bundle --all
git bundle verify /private/audit/recovery.bundle
git bundle list-heads /private/audit/recovery.bundle > /private/audit/refs.txt
```

Use absolute bundle paths: `git -C` interprets relative paths in that repository.
Record a SHA-256 of the bundle, then fetch a representative ref from it into a separate
bare repository and run `git fsck --full` there. A bundle covers committed reachable
objects, not dirty/untracked files or every unreachable object. Save staged/unstaged
binary patches and relevant working files separately; verify their archives. Do not
put bundles, confidential inventories or raw scanner findings in Git or the export.

The deletion manifest is a **preview**, not authorization or an executable script.
Immediately before acting, refresh refs and open PRs, inspect worktree status and check
process cwd/open files. Protect active automation and concurrent work. Use one exact
expected-SHA lease for each ref deletion; stop on any mismatch. Delete in bounded
batches and verify the remaining protected refs after every batch. Never retry a lease
failure with a force deletion. Preserve D/E/F branches until resolved.

Remove worktrees only after inspecting tracked, ordinary untracked **and ignored**
content, checking process use, and preserving their exact HEAD and any useful files.
Save `git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard -z` privately before removal;
ordinary `git status --porcelain` omits ignored logs, scratch files and caches. A clean
status and successful `git worktree remove` do not certify that ignored evidence was
absent or disposable. Preserve useful ignored outputs separately before removal. Missing registrations can be
pruned after recording their HEADs. Keep local-only history until reviewed and backed
up; do not run aggressive garbage collection as housekeeping.

## Finish the operation

The branch creator owns cleanup after merge. Refresh the remote branch tip, confirm
that no new commits, open PR/base, worktree or active process needs it, then remove the
stale ref with an exact lease. If work stops unfinished, leave a bounded handoff with
source SHA, lifecycle, reproduction commands and the remaining review obligation.

Close historical coordination issues when their assigned work is completed. Link the
integration or preservation evidence and explain genuinely outstanding work separately;
new accumulation does not extend an old assignment indefinitely. Preserve intentional
PR drafts. For each unresolved PR, record the exact reviewed head, technical findings,
unique evidence, overlap and next action. Engineering self-review and finite certificate
replay do not satisfy independent mathematical-review requirements.
